{{also|Planet|planèt|plånet}}
==English==
[[File:Planet collage to scale.jpg|thumb|alt=Refer to caption|The eight '''planets''' ({{senseno|en|Q634}}) of the Solar System]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|planete}}, from {{der|en|fro|planete}}, from {{der|en|la|planeta}}, {{m|la|planetes}}, from {{der|en|grc|πλανήτης|t=wanderer}} (itself an {{ellipsis|grc|ἀστέρες πλανῆται|t=wandering stars|nocap=1|nocat=1}}), from {{der|en|grc|πλανάω|t=wander about, stray}}, of unknown origin. Cognate with {{cog|la|pālor|t=wander about, stray}}, {{cog|non|flana|t=to rush about}}, and {{cog|no|flanta|t=to wander about}}. More at {{l|en|flaunt}}. So called because they have apparent motion, unlike the "fixed" stars. Originally including also the moon and sun but not the Earth; modern scientific sense of "world that orbits a star" is from 1630s in English. The Greek word is an enlarged form of {{m|grc|πλάνης|t=who wanders around, wanderer}}, also "wandering star, planet", in medicine "unstable temperature." Displaced native {{ncog|ang|tungol}}.

