===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|now|historical|or|astrology}} Each of the seven major bodies which move relative to the fixed stars in the night sky—the [[Moon]], [[Mercury]], [[Venus]], the [[Sun]], [[Mars]], [[Jupiter]] and [[Saturn]]. {{defdate|from 14<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#* {{RQ:Montaigne Florio Essayes|chapter=12|book=II|page=260|passage=Be they not dreames of humane vanity,{{...}}to make of our knowne earth a bright shining '''planet''' {{transterm|frm|astre}}?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=w:Henry Fielding|title=Tom Jones|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=1973|page=288|passage=The moon{{...}}began to rise from her bed, where she had slumbered away the day, in order to sit up all night. Jones had not travelled far before he paid his compliments to that beautiful '''planet''', and, turning to his companion, asked him if he had ever beheld so delicious an evening?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author={{w|Keith Thomas (historian)|Keith Thomas}}|title=Religion and the Decline of Magic|publisher=Folio Society|year_published=2012|page=361|passage=Another of Boehme's followers, the Welshman Morgan Llwyd, also believed that the seven '''planets''' could be found within man.}}
# {{lb|en|astronomy|historical}} Any body that orbits the Sun, including the [[asteroid]]s (as [[minor planet]]s) and sometimes the [[moon]]s of those bodies (as [[satellite planet]]s)
#: {{syn|en|wandering star|wanderstar}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1640|author=w:John Wilkins|section=title|passage=A Discovrse concerning a New '''Planet'''. Tending to prove, That 'tis probable our Earth is one of the '''Planets'''|title=A Discovrse concerning a New Planet. Tending to prove, That 'tis probable our Earth is one of the Planets}}
# {{senseid|en|Q634}}{{lb|en|astronomy|current}} A body which is massive enough to be in [[hydrostatic equilibrium]] (generally resulting in being an [[ellipsoid]]) but not enough to attain [[nuclear fusion]] and, in [[International Astronomical Union|IAU]] usage, which directly orbits a [[star]] (or multiple star) and dominates the region of its orbit; specifically, in the case of the [[Solar system]], the eight major bodies of [[Mercury]], [[Venus]], [[Earth]], [[Mars]], [[Jupiter]], [[Saturn]], [[Uranus]], and [[Neptune]]. {{defdate|from 2006}}
#: {{syn|en|world}}
#: {{hyper|en|[[planemo]]<qq:in IAU usage>}}
#: {{hypo|en|binary planet|carbide planet|carbon planet|classical planet|diamond planet|double planet|dual planet|[[dwarf planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|exoplanet|extrasolar planet|[[free-floating planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|gas giant|giant planet|hycean planet|ice giant|inferior planet|inner planet |[[interstellar planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|major planet|mesoplanet|[[minor planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|outer planet|[[primary planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|[[rogue planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|[[satellite planet]]<qq:in non-IAU usage>|silicate planet|silicon planet|supergiant planet|superior planet|superplanet|terrestrial planet|water planet}}
#: {{cot|en|brown dwarf|sub-brown dwarf}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2006-12-22|author=Alok Jha|work=w:The Guardian|passage=Their decision will force a rewrite of science textbooks because the solar system is now a place with eight '''planets''' and three newly defined "dwarf '''planets'''"&mdash;a new category of object that includes Pluto.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2009-12-01|author=Keiichi Wada; Yusuke Tsukamoto; Eiichiro Kokubo|title='''Planet''' Formation around Supermassive Black Holes in the Active Galactic Nuclei|journal=w:The Astrophysical Journal|volume=886|issue=2|section=article 107|sectionurl=https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/1538-4357/ab4cf0/pdf}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Ashley Strickland|title=Astronomers discover nearby six-planet solar system with ‘pristine configuration’|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/11/29/world/six-exoplanets-resonance-scn|date=2023-11-29|passage=The closest '''planet''' takes just over nine Earth days to complete an orbit around the star, and the most distant takes about 55 days. All of the '''planets''' have quicker revolutions around their star than Mercury, which takes 88 days to complete one lap around the sun.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260217225959/https://edition.cnn.com/2023/11/29/world/six-exoplanets-resonance-scn|archivedate=17 February 2026}}
# {{construed with|en|[[the]] {{ng|{{nobold|or}}}} [[this]]}}: The [[Earth]].
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|VIII|passage="My tastes," he said, still smiling, "incline me to the garishly sunlit side of this '''planet'''." And, to tease her and arouse her to combat: "I prefer a farandole to a nocturne; I'd rather have a painting than an etching; Mr. Whistler bores me with his monochromatic mud; I don't like dull colours, dull sounds, dull intellects;{{nb...}}."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-07|author=David Simpson
|volume=188|issue=26|page=36|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/books/2013/may/18/falling-upwards-richard-holmes-review Fantasy of navigation]
|passage=It is tempting to speculate about the incentives or compulsions that might explain why anyone would take to the skies in [the] basket [of a balloon]:{{nb...}}; perhaps to moralise on the oneness or fragility of the '''planet''', or to see humanity for the small and circumscribed thing that it is;{{nb...}}.}}

====Usage notes====
* The term ''planet'' originally meant any star which wandered across the sky, and generally included comets and the Sun and Moon. With the Copernican revolution, the Earth was recognized as a planet, and the Sun was seen to be fundamentally different. The Galilean satellites of Jupiter were at first called planets (satellite planets), but later reclassified along with the Moon. The first asteroids were also considered to be planets, but were reclassified when it was realized that there were a great many of them, crossing each other's orbits, in a zone where only a single planet had been expected. Likewise, Pluto was found where an outer planet had been expected, but doubts were raised when it turned out to cross Neptune's orbit and to be much smaller than the expectation required. When [[Eris]], an outer body more massive than Pluto, was discovered, the International Astronomical Union (IAU) officially defined the word ''planet'' as above. However, a significant number of astronomers reject the IAU definition, especially in the field of [[planetary geology]]. Some are of the opinion that orbital parameters should be irrelevant, and that either any equilibrium ([[ellipsoidal]]) body in direct orbit around a star is a planet (there are likely at least a dozen<!--11 universally accepted, not clear if there are more--> such bodies in the Solar system) or that any equilibrium body at all is a planet, thus re-accepting the Moon, the Galilean satellites and other large moons as planets, as well as [[rogue planet]]s.

