# {{lb|en|astronomy|astrophysics}} The [[point]] of a [[body]]'s ([[elliptical]]) [[orbit]] in a 2-body gravitational [[system]] such that the [[distance]] between the two bodies (measured from their respective centres of mass) is at its [[minimum]].
#* '''1983''', Richard O. Fimmel, Laurence Colin, Eric Burgess, ''Pioneer Venus'', NASA SP-461, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=8wbc4tf87h4C&pg=PA109&dq=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjYm9eu45f3AhUot5UCHZqoBk8Q6AF6BAguEAI#v=onepage&q=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&f=false page 109],
#*: ''Figure 5-19. Sun-Venus-Orbiter geometry illustrates how the '''periapsis''' moves around the planet during the Venusian sidereal year to sample day and night hemispheres. Because the planet rotates in a retrograde direction, it takes more than one Venusian sidereal year for '''periapsis''' to move over all longitudes of the planet (as explained in text).''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=D. A. Vallado|title=Fundamentals of Astrodynamics and Applications|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=PJLlWzMBKjkC&pg=PA325&dq=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjYm9eu45f3AhUot5UCHZqoBk8Q6AF6BAg5EAI#v=onepage&q=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&f=false|page=325|publisher=Springer
|passage=We don't always want to burn near '''periapsis'''. Suppose we want to change the height at '''periapsis'''. The most efficient place to change the radius of '''periapsis''' is at apoapsis. Consider applying <math>\Delta v</math> at some other point in the orbit. In this case both '''periapsis''' and apoapsis will change. If we apply the <math>\Delta v</math> tangentially at apoapsis, all of the <math>\Delta v</math> will go into changing '''periapsis''', and vice versa.}}
#* '''2004''', R. S. Saunders, ''et al.'', ''Mars Odyssey Mission'', C. T. Russell (editor), ''2001 Mars Odyssey'', Kluwer Academic Publishers, [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=KogNOXBSUwsC&pg=PA34&dq=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwjYm9eu45f3AhUot5UCHZqoBk8Q6AF6BAhgEAI#v=onepage&q=%22periapsis%22%7C%22periapsides%22&f=false page 34],
#*: The main aerobraking phase began once the point of the spacecraft's '''periapsis''' had been lowered to within 110 km above the Martian surface.
