#* {{quote-book|en|author=Peter Duffett-Smith; Jonathan Zwart|chapter=The Planets, Comets and Binary Stars|title=Practical Astronomy with Your Calculator or Spreadsheet|edition=4th|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=2011|section=section 61 (Comets)|page=143|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=MTGYxQyW998C&pg=PA143|isbn=978-0-521-14654-8|passage=The longitude of the comet is not usually specified at a particular epoch. Rather, the epoch is given when the comet is at '''perihelion''', the point of its closest approach to the Sun. [...] Note that, as in the case of the planetary elements, we have specified ϖ, the longitude of the '''perihelion'''.}}
