==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|phylosopher||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|philosophre}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}} or {{der|en|frm|philosophe}}, from {{der|en|la|philosophus}}, from {{der|en|grc|φιλόσοφος|lit=[[lover]] of [[wisdom]]}} + {{m|en|-er}}.

Credited as having been coined by {{w|Pythagoras}} to describe himself.<ref>Attributed dates to Roman antiquity: {{w|Cicero}}, ''{{w|Tusculan Disputations}}'', 5.3.8-9 = {{w|Heraclides Ponticus}} fr. 88 Wehrli, {{w|Diogenes Laertius}} 1.12, 8.8, {{w|Iamblichus}} ''VP'' 58.</ref><ref>This view has been challenged by {{w|Walter Burkert}}, but it has been defended by C.J. De Vogel, ''Pythagoras and Early Pythagoreanism'' (1966), pp. 97–102, and C. Riedweg, ''Pythagoras: His Life, Teaching, And Influence'' (2005), p. 92.</ref>

Displaced native {{noncog|ang|ūþwita}}.

