===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|[[originally]]}} A lover of wisdom.
#: {{ant|en|misosopher}}
# A student of [[philosophy]].
# A scholar or expert engaged in or contributing to philosophical inquiry.
#: {{hyponyms|en|Thesaurus:philosopher}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1972|author1=w:Mortimer J. Adler|author2=w:Charles Van Doren|title=w:How to Read a Book|text=Not much later—perhaps only two or three weeks later he has discovered meaning in them; he knows that they say "The cat sat on the hat." How this happens no one really knows, despite the efforts of '''philosophers''' and psychologists over two and a half millennia to study the phenomenon.|isbn=0-671-21209-5|lccn=72-81451|page=25|pageurl=https://books.google.co.in/books?id=Z5PpkQadm5EC&pg=PA25&source=gbs_toc_r&cad=2#v=onepage&q&f=false}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=w:Harold Bloom|title=Bloom's Modern Critical Views: Stephen King
|passage=Their playwrights knew better. Scandal, murder, hair-rending and railing against the gods sold tickets. King is not a '''philosopher'''. He knows how to sell tickets.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A person who applies the principles of philosophy to the conduct of their life, as by acting calmly and rationally in the face of inevitable change.
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Acts|17|18|passage=Then certaine '''Philosophers''' of the Epicureans, and of the Stoikes, encountred him}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|text=This is not the sort of happiness which a man would in general wish to owe to his wife; but where other powers of entertainment are wanting, the true '''philosopher''' will derive benefit from such as are given.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} A student, scholar, or expert in any branch of knowledge, especially those branches studied prior to being considered part of pure [[science]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[alchemist]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1813|author=w:Geoffrey Chaucer|title={{w|The Canterbury Tales}}: {{w|The Canon's Yeoman's Tale}}
|passage=Then thus conclude I, since that God of heaven<br />Will not that these '''philosophers''' neven<br />How that a man shall come unto this stone,<br />I rede as for the best to let it gon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1945|author=w:Bertrand Russell|title=w:History of Western Philosophy|text=No further progress was made in this science until the Mohammedan alchemists embarked upon their search for the '''philosopher's''' stone, the elixir of life, and a method of transmuting base metals into gold.}}

